WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.846
Common Python security mistakes usually come from misplaced trust.

2
00:00:03.846 --> 00:00:15.121
Code accepts a value too early, relies on a default too long, assumes a
caller has permission, treats a file as harmless, trusts a dependency
without review, or logs data that should not be widely visible.

3
00:00:15.121 --> 00:00:19.431
These mistakes are rarely about Python being unsuitable for secure
systems.

4
00:00:19.431 --> 00:00:24.339
They are about engineering decisions that need to be explicit, tested, and
maintained.

5
00:00:24.339 --> 00:00:28.053
Strong Python security depends on safe APIs and clear boundaries.

6
00:00:28.053 --> 00:00:31.899
Validate input for type, shape, range, format, and business intent.

7
00:00:31.899 --> 00:00:33.955
Use parameterized database access.

8
00:00:33.955 --> 00:00:35.812
Avoid unsafe dynamic execution.

9
00:00:35.812 --> 00:00:42.046
Treat command execution, templates, parsers, uploaded files, and
deserialization as high-risk boundaries.

10
00:00:42.046 --> 00:00:45.428
Enforce authorization server-side and at the object level.

11
00:00:45.428 --> 00:00:52.657
Protect tenant boundaries and sensitive operations consistently across
normal, bulk, administrative, and background paths.

12
00:00:52.657 --> 00:00:58.294
Secrets, configuration, dependencies, logs, and errors are part of the
product, not side concerns.

13
00:00:58.294 --> 00:01:00.682
Keep secrets out of code, images, and logs.

14
00:01:00.682 --> 00:01:05.059
Review production configuration separately from development convenience.

15
00:01:05.059 --> 00:01:08.573
Manage dependencies deliberately and keep updates testable.

16
00:01:08.573 --> 00:01:13.481
Use logs to support detection and investigation while minimizing sensitive
exposure.

17
00:01:13.481 --> 00:01:18.389
Treat debug output and verbose errors as controlled diagnostics, not
public behavior.

18
00:01:18.389 --> 00:01:21.837
Tools and frameworks help, but they do not replace ownership.

19
00:01:21.837 --> 00:01:31.453
The durable improvement comes from shared patterns, clear review
questions, defensive tests, good defaults, careful dependency management,
and operational feedback.

20
00:01:31.453 --> 00:01:33.045
The goal is not perfect code.

21
00:01:33.045 --> 00:01:39.080
The goal is to reduce repeated mistakes and make secure behavior the
easiest behavior to maintain over time.
